# The engine's JVM options (engine/runner JvmOptions.java), one per line, before
# ENSO_JVM_OPTIONS. The IDE's shim, bin/enso and enso.bat, and the runner's own relaunch
# (Main.launchJvm) all read this one list; the shim parses it as JvmOptions.java does. The
# IDE's per-user AOT cache key includes this file's text, so a change here retrains it
# (project-manager-shim jvmAot/key.ts).
#
# Fixed options. Note that a line is a JVM option in single-token form, not a `java` launcher one.
# Unsafe memory access is used by Truffle and Arrow; JDK 25 warns about it.
--sun-misc-unsafe-memory-access=allow
--enable-native-access=org.graalvm.truffle
--add-opens=java.base/java.nio=ALL-UNNAMED

# JDK 25 warns about these modules' restricted method calls; a later JDK makes them errors.
--enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED,org.enso.language.server

# C2 compiles the engine's own Java, not Graal-as-JIT (JVMCI), which opens a project faster.
# Truffle guest code still compiles through libgraal. See #518.
-XX:-UseJVMCICompiler

# Graal's pre-25.3 inliner: 25.3+ priority inlining doubled JIT CPU while a project
# opens (#518, P). Applies to Truffle's compilations through libgraal.
-Djdk.graal.UsePriorityInlining=false
